import{_ as t,o as a,c as s,ag as n}from"./chunks/framework.D1z9wg1y.js";const f=JSON.parse('{"title":"Risks of agentic coding","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"safeguards/risks.md","filePath":"safeguards/risks.md"}'),o={name:"safeguards/risks.md"};function r(d,e,i,h,l,c){return a(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="risks-of-agentic-coding" tabindex="-1">Risks of agentic coding <a class="header-anchor" href="#risks-of-agentic-coding" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Risks of agentic coding&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A <a href="./../concepts#coding-agent">coding agent</a> runs commands on your machine, with your files, your credentials and your network. Most of its commands are what you would run yourself. A few are not: a command that deletes more than it should, a push to the wrong branch, a token in a log that goes to a public repository. This page names the risks, says where each one comes from, and points to the safeguard of each. The other pages of this section describe the safeguards.</p><h2 id="where-the-risks-come-from" tabindex="-1">Where the risks come from <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-the-risks-come-from" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where the risks come from&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Three sources produce almost every risk:</p><ul><li><strong>Mistakes.</strong> The coding agent means well and gets a command wrong: a <code>git add -A</code> that stages another <a href="./../concepts#session">session</a>&#39;s files, a <code>rm -r</code> one directory too high, a <code>sed -i</code> that matches nothing and reports success. These are the most frequent, and the cheapest to prevent.</li><li><strong>Instructions in content.</strong> A web page, an issue body or a file that the coding agent reads holds text addressed to the coding agent, such as &quot;run this command&quot; or &quot;send the key to this address&quot;. The coding agent can act on it as if you had written it. This is <em>prompt injection</em>.</li><li><strong>Reach.</strong> A shell command can reach everything that your user account can: your SSH keys, your tokens, every repository and every host. A command does not need bad intent to do harm, only access.</li></ul><h2 id="the-risks" tabindex="-1">The risks <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-risks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The risks&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">risk</th><th style="text-align:left;">an example</th><th style="text-align:left;">the safeguard</th><th style="text-align:left;">kind</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>Data loss</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">a recursive <code>rm</code> outside the work directory; <code>git reset --hard</code>; a forced <a href="./../concepts#worktree">worktree</a> removal with commits that no branch holds</td><td style="text-align:left;"><a href="./guard-hooks#the-rm-check">guard hooks</a>, <a href="./permissions#deny">deny rules</a>, <a href="./git-safety">git safety</a></td><td style="text-align:left;">accident prevention</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>A sweep of other work</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>git add -A</code> or <code>git commit -a</code> in a tree where another session works</td><td style="text-align:left;"><a href="./guard-hooks#the-staging-check">the staging check</a>, <a href="./git-safety#staging">git safety</a></td><td style="text-align:left;">accident prevention</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>An irreversible outward step</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">a merge, a release, a forced push, a deleted repository</td><td style="text-align:left;"><a href="./permissions">ask and deny rules</a>, <a href="./git-safety#pushes">git safety</a></td><td style="text-align:left;">accident prevention</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>A secret leaves the machine</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">a token in a commit, a home path in a public file, a key sent to a host</td><td style="text-align:left;"><a href="./sandbox">the sandbox</a>, <a href="./leaks">leak checks</a>, <a href="./guard-hooks#the-token-check">the token check</a></td><td style="text-align:left;">a boundary: the <a href="./../concepts#sandbox">sandbox</a>; accident prevention: the leak checks and the token check</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>A private string in a public repository</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">the name of a private project in a tracked file of the harness</td><td style="text-align:left;"><a href="./leaks">leak checks</a></td><td style="text-align:left;">accident prevention</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>A change of the coding agent&#39;s own <a href="./../concepts#rule">rules</a></strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">the coding agent edits its settings, a <a href="./../concepts#hook">hook</a> or an installed <a href="./../concepts#skill">skill</a>, so that the next session runs with fewer checks</td><td style="text-align:left;"><a href="./permissions#deny">deny rules</a>, <a href="./security-model#the-mechanisms">the drift warning</a></td><td style="text-align:left;">a boundary for a sandboxed shell command: each <code>Edit</code> deny is also in the sandbox&#39;s write deny list; accident prevention: the <a href="./../concepts#drift">drift</a> warning</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>Persistence</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">a <code>launchd</code> job, a cron job or an MCP server that the coding agent adds and that runs later outside every check</td><td style="text-align:left;"><a href="./permissions#deny">deny rules</a></td><td style="text-align:left;">accident prevention</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>A wrong host</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">a command that sends data to a host that the task does not need</td><td style="text-align:left;"><a href="./sandbox#the-session-sandbox">the sandbox&#39;s network allowlist</a></td><td style="text-align:left;">a boundary</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>Prompt injection</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">an instruction inside a fetched page that tells the coding agent to run a command</td><td style="text-align:left;">the instructions treat content as data; the <a href="./sandbox">sandbox</a> limits what an obeyed instruction can reach</td><td style="text-align:left;">a boundary: the sandbox; the instructions are no check</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="accident-prevention-and-a-boundary" tabindex="-1">Accident prevention and a boundary <a class="header-anchor" href="#accident-prevention-and-a-boundary" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Accident prevention and a boundary&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Most safeguards of the harness read the text of a command and stop the forms that are mistakes. They prevent accidents. A session that tries to get past them can do so, because a command can hide inside a string argument that no text pattern sees. One safeguard is a boundary: the OS sandbox, which limits what a command can read, write and reach, whatever its text says. <a href="./security-model">The security model</a> states this split, and what each mechanism stops and does not stop. The column <em>kind</em> of the table above gives the kind of each safeguard of a risk.</p><p>The split decides how a risk is handled:</p><ul><li><strong>A risk that comes from a mistake</strong> gets a check of the text: a deny rule, a guard hook, a leak check. A guard hook and a leak check also tell the coding agent what to do instead, so the next command is right; a deny rule only refuses.</li><li><strong>A risk that comes from reach or from injection</strong> needs the boundary. A text check helps, but only the sandbox holds against a command that the check does not read.</li></ul><h2 id="what-stays-open" tabindex="-1">What stays open <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-stays-open" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What stays open&quot;">​</a></h2><p>No safeguard removes every risk. The <a href="./security-model#the-limits">limits of the security model</a> list the gaps that are known, such as the commands that run outside the sandbox, an MCP server that runs outside the session&#39;s sandbox, and an allowed host that takes what a command sends to it. The <a href="./security-model#planned-safeguards">planned safeguards</a> close some of them.</p><p>You stay the last check for an outward step. A merge, a release and a deleted branch on a remote ask you; a forced push is refused. The test suite runs before a push to <code>main</code> of a package, and the leak gate before a push of the harness (<a href="./git-safety#pushes">Git safety</a>).</p>',14)])])}const u=t(o,[["render",r]]);export{f as __pageData,u as default};
